<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #f0f8ff; border: 2px solid #1a5276; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write short personal narratives about weekend activities. Interactive: Edit simple written work from peers for grammar and spelling collaboratively.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short story about their weekend (e.g., "This weekend we have a special Panamanian meal with healthy food." "I want to cook."). Can check a peer's work and give feedback (e.g., "Not aet, e-a-t, eat."). Can share their weekend plans with the class (e.g., "This weekend, I am...").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a healthy plate with various fruits and vegetables (e.g., mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, rice, beans). Students are asked to write down 3-5 food items they can identify from the picture. Then, they quickly pair up and share their lists with a partner ("I see mangos, rice, and beans.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two simple sentences on the board about the picture, for example: "This plate has many healthy fruits." and "I can eat some tasty vegetables." The teacher reads the sentences aloud, modeling clear pronunciation, especially for /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit." The teacher then points to 'many' and 'some' and explains they tell us about quantities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are mangos countable or uncountable?" (Expected answer: Countable!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'can' mean in 'I can eat'?" (Expected answer: It means I am able to or it's possible.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple narrative excerpt (2-3 sentences) about "My Weekend Meal Plan" written on a large flashcard or projected, with a few blanks for key vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher presents a sample text like:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"This weekend, my family often ______ (cooks/eats) a special ______ (meal). For ______ (lunch), we like to have ______ (rice) and ______ (beans). My mom ______ (slices/cuts) fresh ______ (tomatoes) and ______ (lettuce) for a ______ (healthy) salad. We also enjoy ______ (some) sweet ______ (fruits) like ______ (mangos) or ______ (watermelon)."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads the text aloud and then guides students to fill in the blanks together using words from the vocabulary list (cook, eat, meal, lunch, rice, beans, slice, cut, tomatoes, lettuce, healthy, some, fruits, mangos, watermelon). Teacher writes the correct words in the blanks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand and extract specific information from a short written narrative about meal planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What meal does the family eat on the weekend?" (Expected: lunch)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the family eat healthy food?" (Expected: Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name two fruits mentioned in the story." (Expected: mangos, watermelon)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using Present Simple, modal 'can', countable/uncountable nouns, and target vocabulary related to meals and healthy food. Focus on basic sentence structure and spelling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences. They must choose the correct word from a word bank to complete each sentence, focusing on vocabulary (e.g., 'breakfast', 'cook', 'healthy', 'some', 'many') and grammar (e.g., 'eat' vs. 'eats', 'some rice', 'many mangos').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><i>Example sentences:</i> "1. For ______, I like to eat eggs and bread. (breakfast/lunch)" "2. My mom can ______ tasty rice and beans. (boil/cook)" "3. We need ______ water. (some/many)" "4. Are there ______ apples? (some/many)" "5. Fruits and vegetables are ______ food. (salty/healthy)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - Guided Sentence Writing:</b> The teacher provides sentence starters on the board or a handout, and students complete them with their own ideas about meals and food. They are encouraged to use specific vocabulary (fruits, vegetables, meal names, verbs, quantifiers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><i>Sentence starters:</i> "This weekend, I want to eat..." "For dinner, my family often..." "I can help my mom to cook..." "We always have some..." "Fruits like ______ and ______ are very healthy."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Peer Check for Spelling and Grammar:</b> Students exchange their completed sentences from activity 2 with a partner. They collaboratively check each other's work for simple spelling errors (e.g., "tomatos" vs. "tomatoes") and basic grammar mistakes (e.g., "I like eat" vs. "I like to eat" or "he cook" vs. "he cooks"). They underline errors and suggest corrections. Teacher circulates, providing support and correction as needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written product followed by peer-to-peer editing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to write a short personal narrative (3-5 sentences) titled "My Healthy Weekend Meal Plan." They should describe what they or their family often eats for a specific meal (breakfast, lunch, or dinner) on the weekend, including at least two healthy fruits or vegetables, using verbs like 'cook', 'eat', 'enjoy', 'slice', or 'cut', and quantifiers like 'some' or 'many'. They must use the Present Simple tense and the modal verb 'can'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing their first draft, students exchange their narratives with a new partner. They use a simple peer editing checklist (provided by the teacher) to check their partner's work for:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Is there a title?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Are there at least 3 sentences?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Are there healthy foods (fruits/vegetables)?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Are there any spelling mistakes?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Is the present simple used correctly? (e.g., "I eat", "She cooks")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Is 'some' or 'many' used correctly?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partners provide written feedback directly on the paper (e.g., circling misspelled words, writing "check verb" next to a grammar error). They verbally discuss 1-2 suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then revise their own narratives based on the peer feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during peer editing and collection of final written narratives for review using a simple checklist/rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their revised "My Healthy Weekend Meal Plan" narratives. The teacher assesses the writing and the peer feedback given.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Clarity:</b> Narrative clearly describes a weekend meal with healthy foods. (Can write a short story about their weekend)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Correctly uses at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., breakfast, lunch, dinner, fruits, vegetables, cook, eat, healthy, some, many).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar & Spelling:</b> Mostly correct use of Present Simple and countable/uncountable nouns. Minimal spelling errors.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration (Peer Editing):</b> Provided clear, helpful feedback to their partner (assessed through review of the marked drafts). (Can check a peer's work and give feedback)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Write one new word you used in your story today.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What was one thing your partner helped you correct in your writing?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What part of writing your story was easy/difficult today? Why?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How did working with your partner help your writing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., students were engaged in peer editing, successfully used target vocabulary).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve writing or collaborative editing? (e.g., provide more sentence frames for weaker writers, review specific grammar points in more detail).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will use their written meal plans to create a simple poster or menu, and present their healthy meal choices to the class, integrating their writing into a larger project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>